  Q: [
    {s: 'mech', q: 'Explain the pathogenesis of acute calculous cholecystitis.', a: 'Impacted stone (cystic duct/Hartmann\'s pouch) → bile stasis → lysolecithin (phospholipase on lecithin) mucosal injury + ↑ intraluminal pressure → ischaemia → inflammation → secondary infection (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, anaerobes). Acalculous cholecystitis in the critically ill (ICU, burns, trauma, TPN, sepsis, HIV — CMV/Cryptosporidium).', kw: ['impact|obstruct|cystic duct', 'stasis', 'lysolecithin|phospholipase', 'ischaem|pressure', 'infection|e. coli|e coli', 'acalculous']},
    {s: 'comp', q: 'What complications can occur?', a: 'Empyema (pus), gangrene, perforation (pericholecystic abscess, peritonitis), emphysematous cholecystitis (gas-forming organisms — diabetics), cholecysto-enteric fistula → gallstone ileus, Mirizzi syndrome (stone in Hartmann\'s pouch compresses CHD → jaundice). Chronic: Rokitansky–Aschoff sinuses, fibrosis, porcelain gallbladder (carcinoma risk).', kw: ['empyema', 'gangren|perforat', 'emphysematous|diabet', 'fistula|ileus', 'mirizzi', 'porcelain|rokitansky']},
    {s: 'mgmt', q: 'How is acute cholecystitis managed?', a: 'NPO/IV fluids, analgesia (NSAIDs/opioids), IV antibiotics (e.g. ceftriaxone + metronidazole or co-amoxiclav), early laparoscopic cholecystectomy (within 1 week, ideally 72 h); percutaneous cholecystostomy if too unwell for surgery. HIDA scan if ultrasound equivocal (non-filling gallbladder).', kw: ['iv fluid|npo|nil by mouth', 'analges|nsaid|opioid', 'antibiotic|ceftriaxone|metronidazole', 'cholecystectomy|72|early', 'cholecystostomy']},
  ],
